keywords = {Electric vehicle charging, integration of EVs on grid, smart charging, grid stability, grid operation with EVs, planning with EVs},
abstract = {An increase in greenhouse gas emissions from fossil fuel based power generations and IC engine based transportation systems paves way for exploration of green and environmental friendly energy sources. In this regard, electric vehicles (EVs) play a major role in reducing carbon foot prints from the above sources. Predominantly, EVs, including battery powered vehicle, hybrid EV, plug-in hybrid EVs, and hydrogen powered fuel cell based EVs, will be the preferable mode of transportation in near future and EVs will dominant the transportation sector in upcoming years. They also pose threat to power distribution system with the available charging infrastructure. As discussed, issues related to stability, poor voltage profile, feeder congestion, and overloading prohibit the growth of EV market. The present distribution grid would face challenges if bulk integration of EVs happen at improper nodes of distribution feeder. Hence, with proper management and implementation with Vehicle-to-grid technologies, EVs can be turned out to be a major role player in maintaining environmental sustainability and in transportation. The objective of this chapter is to analyze the effects of EV charging on distribution systems under different factors such as voltage stability, grid reliability, and grid planning. For this purpose different penetration levels of EVs in the grid are considered. To analyze the effects of EV charging, an IEEE 13 bus test feeder with an integrated EV charging system is considered as in Section 12.6. Further, the effect of harmonics which are introduced in the grid because of EV charging is discussed in Section 12.8. Also, an introduction to controlled smart EV charging techniques is presented which is useful in reducing the peak load and minimizing losses on the distribution system.}
}